            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>Dockerfile for ASP.NET Core</h2><p>Use a multi-stage Dockerfile to build and publish your app in the SDK image, then copy the published output into a smaller runtime image for production.</p><pre><code class="language-dockerfile">FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build
WORKDIR /src

COPY *.csproj ./
RUN dotnet restore

COPY . ./
RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /app/publish

FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0 AS runtime
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/publish .

EXPOSE 8080
ENV ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://+:8080

ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "MyApi.dll"]</code></pre></section>
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>Docker Compose</h2><p>Compose lets you define and run multi-container apps (API + database). Keep secrets out of compose files in production; use environment-specific secret stores instead.</p><pre><code class="language-yaml">version: '3.8'
services:
  api:
    build: .
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    environment:
      - ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production
      - ConnectionStrings__Default=Server=db;Database=MyDb;User=sa;Password=YourPassword123!
    depends_on:
      - db

  db:
    image: mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server:2022-latest
    environment:
      - ACCEPT_EULA=Y
      - SA_PASSWORD=YourPassword123!
    ports:
      - "1433:1433"
    volumes:
      - sqldata:/var/opt/mssql

volumes:
  sqldata:</code></pre></section>
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>Build and Run</h2><p>Build images locally for testing, and prefer CI pipelines for production builds and image publishing to a registry.</p><pre><code class="language-bash"># Build the image
docker build -t myapi .

# Run the container
docker run -p 8080:8080 myapi

# With Docker Compose
docker-compose up -d

# View logs
docker-compose logs -f api</code></pre></section>
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>Health Checks</h2><p>Expose health endpoints so orchestration systems (Kubernetes, Docker Swarm) can probe readiness and liveness; include checks for critical dependencies like the database.</p><pre><code class="language-csharp">builder.Services.AddHealthChecks()
    .AddDbContextCheck&lt;AppDbContext&gt;()
    .AddCheck("custom", () => HealthCheckResult.Healthy());

app.MapHealthChecks("/health");
app.MapHealthChecks("/health/ready", new HealthCheckOptions
{
    Predicate = check => check.Tags.Contains("ready")
});</code></pre></section>
                <section class="lesson-section"><h2>Production Configuration</h2><p>Prefer environment variables and external secret stores for production configuration. Configure Kestrel limits to protect resources and set sensible defaults for request sizes.</p><pre><code class="language-csharp">// Use environment variables in production
builder.Configuration
    .AddJsonFile("appsettings.json")
    .AddJsonFile($"appsettings.{builder.Environment.EnvironmentName}.json", optional: true)
    .AddEnvironmentVariables();

// Configure Kestrel
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
{
    options.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 10 * 1024 * 1024; // 10MB
});</code></pre></section>
